To solve this problem, we first need to understand the equation given: $|x-\sqrt[5]{16}|=3$. This equation represents the absolute value of the difference between $x$ and $\sqrt[5]{16}$, which equals $3$.

The absolute value equation $|a|=b$ means that $a=b$ or $a=-b$. Applying this to our equation, we get two separate equations:
1. $x - \sqrt[5]{16} = 3$
2. $x - \sqrt[5]{16} = -3$

Solving each equation for $x$ gives us the endpoints of the segment on the number line.

For the first equation:
\begin{align*}
x - \sqrt[5]{16} &= 3 \\
x &= 3 + \sqrt[5]{16}
\end{align*}

For the second equation:
\begin{align*}
x - \sqrt[5]{16} &= -3 \\
x &= -3 + \sqrt[5]{16}
\end{align*}

So, the endpoints of the segment are $-3 + \sqrt[5]{16}$ and $3 + \sqrt[5]{16}$.

The length of the segment is the distance between these two points, which is the difference between their values:
\begin{align*}
\text{Length} &= (3 + \sqrt[5]{16}) - (-3 + \sqrt[5]{16}) \\
&= 3 + \sqrt[5]{16} + 3 - \sqrt[5]{16} \\
&= 6
\end{align*}

Therefore, the length of the segment is $\boxed{6}$.
